<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William I of Sicily</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1131–1166</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as William the Bad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, called the Bad, was King of Sicily from 1154 to 1166, succeeding his father Roger II. His reign faced baronial revolts and a Byzantine invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, which he defeated in 1156. During his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> took Roger II's North African possessions, ending with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> in January 1160.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was the fourth son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> and Elvira of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, grandson of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-i-of-sicily">Roger I of Sicily</a> and great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a>. He grew up with little expectation of ruling. The deaths of his three older brothers, Roger, Tancred and Alfonso, between 1138 and 1148 changed matters, though he was still poorly prepared when his father died. His epithet, the Bad, seems little merited and expresses the bias of the historian Hugo Falcandus and of the baronial class against the king and the official class who guided him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>William kept his father's administration and promoted the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maio-of-bari">Maio of Bari</a>, a man of low birth with the title ammiratus ammiratorum, who held real power at first. Maio continued Roger's policy of excluding much of the nobility from central administration.</p><p>On the mainland, however, royal authority still depended on the cooperation of counts and barons in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and the Terra di Lavoro. They were encouraged to revolt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-iv">Pope Adrian IV</a>, the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Comnenus</a> and the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I. At the end of 1155 Byzantine troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> and other towns. William landed and destroyed the Byzantine fleet and army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brindisi">Brindisi</a> on 28 May 1156, and by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> of 18 June 1156 Adrian abandoned the rebels and confirmed him as king.</p><p>The African dominions were lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. A revolt began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sfax">Sfax</a> in 1156 and nothing was done to put it down, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> surrendered on 11 January 1160, ending the African empire. In November 1160 Maio was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> by Matthew Bonello. The royal palace was then stormed, and the king and his family were captured, but the people and the army rallied round him. He recovered power, had Bonello blinded and reduced the rest of the kingdom. His son Roger was killed by a wayward arrow, though Falcandus has the king kicking him dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Afterwards William entrusted the government to a triumvirate of Matthew of Ajello, Count Sylvester of Marsico and Bishop Palmer of Syracuse. His last years were peaceful, and he supported the true pope against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, so that Alexander III was installed in the Lateran Palace in November 1165 by a guard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>. He died on 7 May 1166 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-cathedral">Palermo Cathedral</a>, later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monreale-cathedral">Monreale Cathedral</a> by his son and heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-sicily">William II of Sicily</a>. By his wife Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> he had four sons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William I of Sicily” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pecheneg troops still fought for Byzantium: at Otranto in 1105 or 1106 against Bohemond, and for Manuel I Komnenos in southern Italy against <b>William the Bad</b>, some of them at the Battle of Andria in 1155.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.12,13.36&amp;year=1153" aria-label="Palermo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of William I of Sicily"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5891 4256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5891 4256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.12,13.36&amp;year=1153">What was here in 1153? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
